    "evidence_text": " It was likely a vassal state of both the Majapahit Empire and the Siamese at different times in the 14th century.\n\n During the early 14th century, Halebidu was sacked and looted by Muslim invaders from northern India and the temple fell into a state of ruin and neglect.\n\n Halebidu is historically important as the erstwhile capital of the Hoysala empire.\n\nHalebidu\n\nHalebeedu (literally \"old capital\") is a town located in Hassan District, Karnataka, India.\n\n Halebidu (which used to be called Dorasamudra or Dwarasamudra) was the regal capital of the Hoysala Empire in the 12th century.\n\n The city got the name \"Halebidu\" because it was damaged and deserted into \"old capital\" after being ransacked and looted twice by north Indian Muslim armies of the Delhi Sultanate in the 14th-century.\n\n Hoysala influence was at its peak in the 13th century, when it dominated the Southern Deccan Plateau region.",
